    "narrative": "The path to victory today for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by two high-efficiency upside plays: **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) and **James Wood** (75.25). **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a quintessential plus-matchup against Avila; the 14% dynamic platoon boost and 14% park factor elevation make him an elite start despite the modest pedigree. Similarly, **James Wood** is set to feast against the southpaw P\u00e9rez; his elite xwOBA of 0.424 combined with a 16% SP skill adjustment creates a massive ceiling in the leadoff spot. We are also leaning on **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.21) and **Jose Caballero** (66.64) to provide the necessary category glue, with **Jose Caballero** notably overcoming a poor lineup position (Order #9) due to a favorable matchup against Peterson and the inherent speed profile we covet.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today involved keeping **Isaac Paredes** (48.51) on the bench. While his dynamic platoon edge against right-handed pitching is valuable, his efficiency was severely throttled by the presence of deGrom on the mound, which triggered a massive 19% downward adjustment to his SP skill score. He simply could not displace the likes of **Austin Riley** (65.85) or **Seiya Suzuki** (61.38), both of whom offer more favorable projected outcomes. **Dalton Rushing** remains glued to the pine with a sub-40 efficiency score, as the combination of a difficult matchup against Tolle and a bottom-tier lineup placement puts him well below our operational floor.\n\nManagement must remain vigilant regarding the volatile nature of the day's slate. While the offensive projections are generally favorable, we are facing some high-end talent that could deflate our daily totals. We are strictly monitoring **Corbin Carroll** (62.09) and **Gabriel Moreno** (50.40); both face Messick, an arm that induces a 6% SP skill penalty. While **Corbin Carroll** is a locked-in core piece due to his speed profile, his performance against Messick will 